tree clean\n86c03af stuck-watch: a dead red-dirty lane with commits ahead is stuck too\n5c446c5 board: dispatch float-build-go into free lane\n2767a26 board: escalate stdin-redesign-build (issue-159) after 4 runs, gate redispatch\nafa7de2 board: dispatch float-build-python into free lane\n245bfcc board: dispatch float-build-rust into free lane\nAvailable recipes:\n    check          # The fast inner loop: skips the docs mega-test, the suite's ~137s long pole. Gate on `just test`.\n    checks         # The repo's mechanical checks, the same surface the Stop hook runs.\n    clippy         # Run clippy with the repo's lint set, the same surface the Stop hook checks.\n    default        # List available recipes.\n    drive          # The autonomous drive loop: a tick every 600s, live colorized output, Ctrl-C to stop. Run ONE.\n    euler-data DIR # `just test`. DIR holds your own copies of the raw data texts; fails loudly, never skips.\n    fmt            # Formatter check over every .toy file from the repo root down (exit 1 on drift).\n    fmt-write      # Rewrite drifted files in place (same exit code as the check).\n    peek           # Watch the current coordinator tick live (detaches with Ctrl-C, tick untouched).\n    slow-test      # this is where the deferred execution claim is re-verified.\n    test           # The final gate: the full test suite. Landing requires this green.\n    tick           # Fire one coordinator tick right now (zero tokens if there is nothing to do).\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"git status && git log --oneline -5 && just --list","time":{"start":1788374622090,"end":1788374622093}},"id":"prt_0636f1ddf001b59UXZ5f6nBUMj","sessionID":"ses_f9c90f27fffeQr9sSyaGoxWpAt","messageID":"msg_0636f0e8b001WxwneDMxnMTr6e"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788374622448,"sessionID":"ses_f9c90f27fffeQr9sSyaGoxWpAt","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-931a8c8117219516","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"gh issue view 172"},"output":"title:\tRetire the three stdin keywords for one Stream<Str> source, parse(.) as a library call (re-file of gh:159)\nstate:\tOPEN\nauthor:\tkantord (Dániel Kántor)\nlabels:\t\ncomments:\t0\nassignees:\t\nprojects:\t\nmilestone:\t\nissue-type:\t\nparent:\t\nsub-issues:\t\nsub-issues-completed:\t\nblocked-by:\t\nblocking:\t\nnumber:\t172\n--\nRe-filed replacement for #159 (maintainer ruling, 2026-09-02: drop the poisoned `issue-159` lane rather than keep repairing it -- delete the old board row, re-board fresh).\n\n**Why re-filed rather than continued:** the feature itself landed cleanly in the `issue-159` lane's first run (commit `45c76be`), but three subsequent repair attempts against a 16-file merge conflict with `main` each failed, leaving that branch uncompilable (unclosed delimiter, `src/check/mod.rs:3034`, 177 dirty files). The worktree/branch are abandoned in place rather than force-cleaned (`git worktree remove --force` / `git branch -D` are both denied to the drive-loop coordinator by the permission classifier). A fresh issue number gets a fresh lane (`issue-<N>`) that never touches that poisoned state.\n\n## Ruling (unchanged from #159)\n\nFrom the call-syntax-and-outside-world grill round (stdin-syntax-design board row), tentative per the maintainer's own hedge (\"not totally certain but I'm leaning towards this\"): **one Stream<Str> source, parse as a library call** -- retiring `input`, `inputs`, and `lines` for a single raw source (working name `stdin`), with today's `input`/`inputs` behavior expressed as an ordinary `parse(.)` call over it.\n\nShape ratified (from the round's preview, PROPOSED/unverified):\n\n```toylang\nfn total(nums: Vec<Int>) -> Int = length(nums)\ntotal(collect(stdin | map(parse(.))))\n```\n\nGiven the hedge, and given the round itself named \"verify type-flow's reach before deciding\" as a live alternative, the build should start with the smallest possible proof that `parse(.)` inside a `map` body actually resolves an expected type end-to-end -- the premise `type-flow-rework` (gh:44) was assumed, not directly re-verified, when this ruling was made.\n\n**New for this re-file:** the prior lane's failures were all merge-conflict repair, not implementation -- the underlying feature diff (commit `45c76be` on the abandoned `issue-159` branch) can be read for reference on what already worked, but should be re-derived against current `main` rather than cherry-picked wholesale, since 16 core compiler files have since diverged.\n\nWritten by the drive-loop coordinator session.\n","metadata":{"output":"title:\tRetire the three stdin keywords for one Stream<Str> source, parse(.) as a library call (re-file of gh:159)\nstate:\tOPEN\nauthor:\tkantord (Dániel Kántor)\nlabels:\t\ncomments:\t0\nassignees:\t\nprojects:\t\nmilestone:\t\nissue-type:\t\nparent:\t\nsub-issues:\t\nsub-issues-completed:\t\nblocked-by:\t\nblocking:\t\nnumber:\t172\n--\nRe-filed replacement for #159 (maintainer ruling, 2026-09-02: drop the poisoned `issue-159` lane rather than keep repairing it -- delete the old board row, re-board fresh).\n\n**Why re-filed rather than continued:** the feature itself landed cleanly in the `issue-159` lane's first run (commit `45c76be`), but three subsequent repair attempts against a 16-file merge conflict with `main` each failed, leaving that branch uncompilable (unclosed delimiter, `src/check/mod.rs:3034`, 177 dirty files). The worktree/branch are abandoned in place rather than force-cleaned (`git worktree remove --force` / `git branch -D` are both denied to the drive-loop coordinator by the permission classifier). A fresh issue number gets a fresh lane (`issue-<N>`) that never touches that poisoned state.\n\n## Ruling (unchanged from #159)\n\nFrom the call-syntax-and-outside-world grill round (stdin-syntax-design board row), tentative per the maintainer's own hedge (\"not totally certain but I'm leaning towards this\"): **one Stream<Str> source, parse as a library call** -- retiring `input`, `inputs`, and `lines` for a single raw source (working name `stdin`), with today's `input`/`inputs` behavior expressed as an ordinary `parse(.)` call over it.\n\nShape ratified (from the round's preview, PROPOSED/unverified):\n\n```toylang\nfn total(nums: Vec<Int>) -> Int = length(nums)\ntotal(collect(stdin | map(parse(.))))\n```\n\nGiven the hedge, and given the round itself named \"verify type-flow's reach before deciding\" as a live alternative, the build should start with the smallest possible proof that `parse(.)` inside a `map` body actually resolves an expected type end-to-end -- the premise `type-flow-rework` (gh:44) was assumed, not directly re-verified, when this ruling was made.\n\n**New for this re-file:** the prior lane's failures were all merge-conflict repair, not implementation -- the underlying feature diff (commit `45c76be` on the abandoned `issue-159` branch) can be read for reference on what already worked, but should be re-derived against current `main` rather than cherry-picked wholesale, since 16 core compiler files have since diverged.\n\nWritten by the drive-loop coordinator session.\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"gh issue view 172","time":{"start":1788374622441,"end":1788374622445}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me start by reading AGENTS.md and looking at the issue. The brief says to read AGENTS.md first (already shown in the system prompt, but let me confirm), then `gh issue view 172`.\n\nLet me start.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0636f1cfd001jFfFEO2I0Iq3oj","sessionID":"ses_f9c90f27fffeQr9sSyaGoxWpAt","messageID":"msg_0636f0e8b001WxwneDMxnMTr6e"}}
